## DESCRIPTION
##   Estimating directional derivatives
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Calculus - multivariable)
## DBchapter(Differentiation of multivariable functions)
## DBsection(Directional derivatives and the gradient)
## Date(10/15/09)
## Institution(Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Paul Pearson)
## MLT(ChangeDirection)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hughes-Hallett')
## EditionText1('4')
## Section1('14.4')
## Problem1('78')
## TitleText2('Calculus')
## AuthorText2('Hughes-Hallett')
## EditionText2('5')
## Section2('14.4')
## Problem2('78')
## KEYWORDS('Gradient', 'Directional Derivative')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Vector");

$a      = random(2, 6);
$answer = Compute("$a / sqrt(2)");

BEGIN_PGML
At a certain point on a heated metal plate, the greatest rate of temperature increase, [$a] degrees Celsius per meter, is toward the northeast.  If an object at this point moves directly north, at what rate is the temperature increasing?

    [_]{$answer} degrees Celsius per meter
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Let [`T`] be the temperature function and [`P`] the point. 
 The value of the  greatest rate of increase is the magnitude of the gradient.
Thus [` \left| \nabla T (P) \right| = [$a] `].   
The gradient points in the northeast direction, so 
[` \nabla T (P) = \left \langle [$a] \cos (45^o) , [$a] \sin (45^o) \right
\rangle = \left \langle \frac{[$a]}{\sqrt{2}}, \frac{[$a]}{\sqrt{2}} \right \rangle
`].   
A unit vector in the north direction is [` \mathbf{u} = \langle 0, 1 \rangle `], so the rate of change of increase of the temperature in that direction is
[``` D_{\mathbf u} T(P) = \nabla T (P) \cdot \mathbf{u} = \frac{[$a]}{\sqrt{2}} ```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
